 ],
 "literal": "By the garland on Kōtai's chest, and / by the garland of those who embraced Kōtai, and / by the blue water lilies blossomed in the great backwater, / smells of fragrance indeed, the beautiful seashore grove of Toṇṭi. / That is our town; he is our king. / Will he think of us also, oh wise-tongued bard, / at the time of excelling in battle, your / fame-excelling one, 'we saw him', so saying?",
 "translation": "The beautiful seashore grove of Tondi smells of sweet fragrance—from the garland on Kōtai's chest, from the garlands of those who have embraced Kōtai, and from the blue water lilies blooming in the vast backwaters. That is our town, and he is our king. Oh wise-tongued bard, when you go to him, tell him, 'We saw the one whose fame excels when he triumphs in battle.' Will he then remember us?",
 "modern": "கோதையின் மார்பில் உள்ள மாலையாலும், கோதையைத் தழுவியவர்களின் மாலையாலும், பெரிய உப்பங்கழியில் மலர்ந்த நெய்தல் மலராலும், அழகிய கடற்கரைச் சோலையாகிய தொண்டி நறுமணம் வீசுகின்றது. அதுவே எமது ஊர்; அவனே எமது தலைவன். அறிவுடைய இரவலனே! போர்க்களத்தில் வென்று மேம்படும் காலத்தில், உன்னுடைய புகழால் மேம்பட்ட அவனைக் கண்டோம் என்று நீ கூறினால், அவன் எம்மையும் நினைத்துப் பார்ப்பானோ?",
 "explanation": "The poet Poykaiyār is speaking to a wise bard who is on his way to visit the Chera king Cēramāṉ Kōkkōtai Mārpaṉ. The poet describes the rich, fragrant port city of Tondi, which belongs to the king, and tells the bard that Tondi is their town and Kōtai is their king. The fragrance of Tondi is poetically attributed to three sources: the garland on the king's chest, the garlands of his close companions, and the natural blue water lilies of the backwaters. This hyperbole emphasizes the king's grandeur and the prosperity of his land. The poet asks the bard to remind the king of them when he praises the king's triumphs in battle.",
